WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.249
Before their hit movie came out in 2014, Marvel's
Guardians of the Galaxy were obscure comic

00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:07.669
characters known only to hardcore fans.

00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:11.320
Now, Groot, Drax, and Rocket Raccoon are household
names.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.190
But what many fans may not know is that some
of the characters underwent drastic changes

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.900
in their transition to the big screen.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.480
So hold on to your robot legs, because here's
what the Guardians of the Galaxy are really

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.220
like…

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.220
"Here you go."

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.850
"Oh, I was just kidding about the leg.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.440
I just needed these two things."

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:27.440
"What"

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.100
"No, I thought it would be funny.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:28.720
Was it funny?"

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.240
The team

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.660
The movie depicts the meeting and initial
team-up of the Guardians of the Galaxy: Star-Lord,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.821
Rocket Raccoon, Groot, Gamora, and Drax the
Destroyer.

00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:41.980
But when the original Guardians of the Galaxy
team debuted in an issue of Marvel Super Heroes

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.950
in 1969, none of those characters were present.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:51.530
Instead, the Guardians consisted of guys named
Vance Astro, Martinex, Charlie-27, and Yondu

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:55.760
— the only one who made it into the first
movie, and not even as a member of the team.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.080
Rocket Raccoon

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.300
In the comics, Rocket Raccoon looks pretty
much the same, but he has a totally different

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.300
back story.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.960
Instead of a soldier of fortune with a tree
sidekick, Rocket originally was the sheriff

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.579
on a planet designated as an insane asylum.

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:12.549
It was in that capacity that he was introduced
to Marvel readers when The Incredible Hulk

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:14.530
accidentally ended up on his planet.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.630
That incident gave Rocket the only sidekick
even less articulate than Groot.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.380
"Puny God."

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.060
Groot

00:01:31.749 --> 00:01:35.530
In a movie full of memorable lines, Groot
has perhaps the most memorable.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.899
"I am Groot."

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:37.899
"So What"

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:39.630
It's pretty much all he can say.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:44.170
But in his earliest comics appearances, Groot
spoke often…and with much foreboding.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.479
In Tales to Astonish #13, he visited Earth
to warn humanity that it's doomed, declaring

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:53.590
himself the ruler of Planet X, and turning
a forest into his own army.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.799
Nearly 50 years later, Groot was re-introduced
in a way that was much closer to the loveable

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:03.299
giant you see on screen, emphasis on the "giant:"
in the comics, he's often closer to 25 feet

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:03.799
tall.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.200
"Ahhhhh"

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.440
Drax

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.530
Portrayed by the almost inhumanly fit MMA
fighter Dave Bautista, Drax is a violence

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.800
machine who doesn't understand the nuance
of language and is singularly focused on one

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.620
goal: avenging the death of his family, who
were killed by Ronan the Accuser.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.270
"Ronan the Accuser."

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.620
"You are the one who transmitted the message."

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.350
"You killed my wife.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.500
You killed my daughter."

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.170
In the comics, his origin is similar, except
it was Thanos who killed both Drax himself

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:46.170
and his family before Drax was resurrected
by Thanos' own dad in order to exact revenge.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.280
Plus, he often rocked a cape, purple pants,
and that adorable little swim cap.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.920
Wouldn't it be great to see him put that outfit
on in a sequel?

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.140
"Yes, Ha Ha Ha."

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.140
Yondu

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.340
In the comics, Yondu was a noble alien archer
who came from the future in an alternate timeline.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.950
In the movies, he's still got the arrow, but
now he's ditched the fantasy threads and gone

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.780
for a decidedly more utilitarian look.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.040
He's also now a rough-around-the-edges intergalactic
pirate who uses a magic arrow to steal, pillage,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.040
and kill.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.200
"He He He"

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.240
If you think that makes him sound like a blue,
kinda evil Hawkeye, you're not entirely wrong

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.240
— but Yondu's arrow is different because
it responds to his commands when he whistles…

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.540
...which is, y'know, pretty cool.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.020
Peter Quill

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.990
The movie version of Peter Quill is equal
parts thief, pirate, and salvager, all of

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:49.100
which is a far cry from his original style
in the comics: a square-jawed, law-abiding

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.540
space cop with a skintight unitard.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:56.310
Though now the de facto leader of the Guardians,
he didn't appear in the comics until 1976

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.760
— seven years after the Guardians of the
Galaxy debuted.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.060
But as in the movie, he once did serve time
in the prison known as the Kyln—although

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.320
in the comics, he was locked up alongside
none other than Thanos.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:16.480
"I found something inside of myself, something
incredibly heroic ."

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.499
Gamora

00:04:17.499 --> 00:04:22.340
Rescued by Thanos after aliens killed her
entire race, and trained to become the ultimate

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.740
assassin, Gamora's tragic backstory from the
comics was made even more tragic by director

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.270
James Gunn — because it's Thanos himself
who murders her family in the film version.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.509
"You alienated my favorite daughter Gamora."

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:38.879
But Gunn did go a long way to empower Gamora
in another way, redesigning Zoe Saldana's

00:04:38.879 --> 00:04:43.949
costume so she looks more like a powerful
warrior rather than a typical comic book swimsuit

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:44.999
model.

00:04:44.999 --> 00:04:46.599
Ronan the Accuser

00:04:46.599 --> 00:04:51.639
He's a deeply chilling, hammer-wielding villain
bent on victory at any cost in the Guardians

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:52.940
of the Galaxy movie.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.110
But in the comics he's a bit more complicated,
not to mention jacked up: the Supreme Accuser

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:02.039
of the Kree Empire, Ronan is a career military
man, fighting wars and delivering judgment

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:04.570
according to a strict internal moral code.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:05.870
How complex is he?

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:10.770
Not only has he fought alongside Marvel heroes
at times, Star-Lord actually once served as

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.060
his military advisor.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.400
And, more importantly, his dance partner!

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.260
"Dance off, bro.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:17.080
Me vs you"

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:18.520
"It's on."

00:05:28.009 --> 00:05:29.469
Nebula

00:05:29.469 --> 00:05:34.039
Karen Gillan, the actress cast to play Nebula,
actually bears a bit of a resemblance to the

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:36.810
Nebula of the comics — minus the blue skin,
of course.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.289
But that's why it's a little weird that the
filmmakers made her bald to play the role.

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:45.680
There are other differences, too: in the movie,
Nebula is a loyal servant of Ronan the Accuser,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.250
but in the comics, she's fought alongside
Gamora and against both Ronan and Thanos.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:54.090
Plus, she's doesn't share Professor X's hairline,
so there's that.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.349
Korath

00:05:55.349 --> 00:06:01.259
In both the comics and movie, Korath is gruff,
intense, and focused on the task at hand:

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:03.629
war, combat, and destruction.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.389
As a sidekick in the movie, though, we don't
really get to see much more of him, including

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:10.669
the fact that, in the comics, he's actually
a brilliant scientist.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:15.150
Instead, we mostly have to judge him by his
cool fashion makeover: gone are the goofy

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.050
hat and blue skin of his comic book look,
in favor of Djimon Honsou in futuristic barrettes

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.129
and spooky contact lenses.

00:06:22.129 --> 00:06:23.129
The Collector

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:27.569
Benicio del Toro makes brief appearances in
Thor: The Dark World and Guardians of the

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:29.930
Galaxy as the mysterious Collector.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:34.409
While the scenes are played mostly for comedy,
they also set up Marvel's franchise-wide Infinity

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:35.409
Stones plotline.

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:39.770
See, there's a reason he's collecting everything
cool in the universe: to protect them from

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:40.770
Thanos.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.409
"He's kinda like Noah from Noah's ark.

00:06:43.409 --> 00:06:51.021
He believes that there is gonna be a big catastrophe
in the galaxy and the whole universe and he

00:06:51.021 --> 00:06:55.939
is collecting all these creatures to preserve
them somehow."

00:06:55.939 --> 00:06:59.840
Acting on that need to preserve, the Collector
even tried to "collect" the Avengers in the

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:00.840
comics.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.840
One thing he collected when he went from page
to screen: a bit of a better fashion sense.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.440
I mean, look at this guy...yikes.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.120
Thanos

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:14.199
Thanos pretty much looks exactly the same
in the movies as he does in the comics, which

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:17.479
is like a sinister wad of grape Bubble Yum
with teeth.

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:22.099
In Guardians of the Galaxy, Gamora's desire
to stop her adoptive father is a major reason

00:07:22.099 --> 00:07:24.690
why the team is looking to protect and retrieve
the Infinity Stone.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:29.660
But while he's still a genocidal nihilist
in the comics, he's also a better dad, even

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:33.839
giving Gamora nice Christmases when she was
a little kid with the tree, presents — the

00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:34.839
whole shebang.

00:07:34.839 --> 00:07:39.160
He may be trying to destroy all life in the
universe, but at least he's into hands-on

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.210
parenting.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:45.499
"Fine, I'll do it myself."

00:07:45.499 --> 00:07:46.820
That's the spirit!

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.800
You will do it yourself!

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.039
It's tough being a single dad, you guys.

00:07:51.039 --> 00:07:52.039
Thanks for watching!

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:55.749
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:55.749 --> 00:07:58.689
And leave us a comment to let us know what
you think of the changes Marvel made to the

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:00.050
Guardians of the Galaxy…

